                <pb facs="0676" n="576" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg576"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">251.11 Insull, Samuel/385: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Fish</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-03-18T19:00:00-05:00">March 18, 1934—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>9. Your 5, March 18, 6 p.m., and Department’s 8, March 18, 2 p.m. Since Insull’s
                    landing in Egypt would probably lead to his early return to the United States
                    under the most favorable circumstances as regards his prosecution, the
                    Department does not wish you to give the Egyptian authorities any reason to
                    believe that, by preventing his landing, they would be accommodating this
                    Government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
